What is here. These files are the ZX Spectrum Next FPGA source (https://gitlab.com/SpectrumNext/ZX_Spectrum_Next_FPGA, directory
cores/zxnext/src), commit b047011 (2025-11-06, "CORE VERSION 3.02.03 (Issue 2 untested)"), copied unchanged by tools/vendor_zxnext.sh
(SHA256SUMS lists them): zxnext.vhd and the directories audio, cpu (the Z80N), device (DMA, Copper, CTC, DivMMC, Multiface, interrupt
controller), rom, serial, misc and video (ULA, Layer 2, LoRes, sprites, tilemap). The copyright lines of Alvin Albrecht, Victor Trucco,
Fabio Belavenuto, Garry Lancaster, Charlie Ingley and the others, and the "GNU General Public License ... version 3 of the License, or (at your
option) any later version" headers, are in the files; COPYING is the licence text, GNU GPL version 3, as the upstream repository ships it.

What is not here: the board tops and pin files (zxnext_top_issue*.vhd, *.xdc, *.ucf), the PLLs, the PS/2 keyboard and mouse blocks, the flash-boot
blocks, the HDMI and VGA stages, the Xilinx IP cores (which the upstream keeps as .xcix files outside its source tree) and rtl/ram (written
again in cores/zxnext/ip/zxn_ip.vhd, ours). Neither a ROM nor any NextZXOS content is here or ever goes into this repository, a bench, a package
or a .cor: the Next's ROMs and its operating system arrive on the user's own SD card image (T116 Q4).

rom/bootrom.vhd is the one binary-like file: the 8 KB boot ROM of the FPGA core, machine generated VHDL data ("generated with romgen v3.0 by
MikeJ"), the code the Z80N runs at power on to find the card and load TBBLUE.FW from it. The file carries no licence header of its own; it is part
of the upstream repository's source tree under the repository's GPL v3 COPYING (cores/zxnext/COPYING), which is why it stays. It is not NextZXOS and
not a Spectrum ROM; tools/check_zxnext_tree.py knows it by hash (SHA256SUMS) and refuses any other image-like file under cores/zxnext.

What is built from them is NOT these files. cores/zxnext/gen is what tools/zxnext_gen.py makes of them: three edits to the VHDL in a scratch copy
(rtl/ is never edited; each one is a place the converter does not accept, counted by the generator), the conversion by GHDL to one Verilog file, and
every clocked process put into the clock-enable form (the machine's five related clocks become enables on the partition's 100 MHz clock).
tools/zxnext_gen.py --check keeps rtl/ on SHA256SUMS and gen/ on rtl/.

What the licence means here. GPL v3 covers the gateware of this core and everything linked into its partial bitstream. A .cor of this core that is
given to a third party has to come with its corresponding source: that is this directory (the upstream copy), cores/zxnext/gen (the generated
source), cores/zxnext/ip, cores/zxnext/trx and cores/zxnext/layer2_zxnext.v (our glue), tools/zxnext_gen.py and tools/vendor_zxnext.sh (the
generator), and the build recipe cores/zxnext/core_sources.tcl - all of it is in this repository. The host (layer 1), the other cores and the applications are separate works in separate partial bitstreams, link nothing of this
core and are not covered; host/ is not part of this core's build.
